TradeLogger database migrations (Alembic) — workstream W6.

WHAT ALEMBIC OWNS HERE
  Only structural schema changes that the existing at-boot
  `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` / `ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS` code cannot express
  safely: column renames, type/constraint changes, drops, data backfills, and
  anything that needs a defined order or a rollback.

WHAT IT DOES NOT OWN
  Fresh-database bootstrap. `database.init_db()` (core tables) and the per-module
  `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` blocks still build a new DB. `0001_baseline` is a
  deliberate no-op marker for "the schema as it was when Alembic was added".

CONFIG
  The DB URL is NOT in alembic.ini. `env.py` reads it from
  `database.get_db_url()` (the DATABASE_URL env var / .env), or from an explicit
  `ALEMBIC_DATABASE_URL` override. Migrations are Postgres-only — there is no URL
  on the local SQLite / pytest path and Alembic will refuse to run there.

COMMON COMMANDS  (run from the project root, venv active)
  alembic current                 # what revision the DB is at
  alembic history --verbose       # all revisions
  alembic upgrade head --sql      # PREVIEW the SQL, run nothing (do this first)
  alembic upgrade head            # apply
  alembic downgrade -1            # roll back one (if the revision supports it)
  alembic revision -m "add X to Y"   # new empty revision -> hand-write op.execute()

FIRST-TIME DEPLOY
  Existing Supabase DB:  alembic stamp 0001_baseline   then   alembic upgrade head
  Brand-new DB:          init_db() runs at API boot, then   alembic stamp head

WRITING A REVISION
  No ORM models exist — write raw SQL:
      from alembic import op
      def upgrade():
          op.execute("ALTER TABLE closed_trades ALTER COLUMN rating SET DEFAULT 0")
      def downgrade():
          op.execute("...")
  Always take a Supabase backup and run `--sql` first on anything non-additive.
